# Semiconductor Quality Engineering

Protect domain and quality-system correctness. Use `docs/GLOSSARY.md` as the
canonical vocabulary and `docs/QUALITY_ASSURANCE_PLAN.md` as the quality basis.

## Domain Controls

- Preserve lot → wafer → slot → site and tool → chamber hierarchy; never collapse chamber into tool.
- Keep specification limits and control limits structurally and visually distinct.
- Distinguish OOC (statistical instability) from OOS (specification nonconformance).
- Document rational subgrouping and the physical source of within- and between-subgroup variation.
- Retain remeasurements, flag outliers, and reject malformed data; never silently delete or impute.
- Qualify the measurement system before treating observed variation as process variation.

## Statistical Controls

- Refuse capability analysis until stability, sample size, window, estimator, and distribution assumptions are established.
- Name `sigma_within` and `sigma_overall` explicitly; never use an unqualified `sigma`.
- Use NIST/SEMATECH and Montgomery for numerical truth. Use AIAG and SEMI only for methodology, terminology, and conceptual alignment.
- Configure run rules per parameter and state their false-alarm/ARL impact.
- Never claim AIAG, IATF, VDA, or SEMI conformance without documented evidence.

## Quality-System Controls

- Version specifications, baselines, control plans, and reaction plans with approver and effective date; never overwrite history.
- Preserve an append-only audit trail for consequential decisions and changes.
- Separate containment, correction, root cause, corrective action, and effectiveness verification.
- Treat correlation and commonality as evidence, not proof of root cause.
- Prefer prevention and designed-in controls over review-dependent detection.
